  "response": "{\n  \"primary_cause\": \"insufficient_state_action_coverage\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.88,\n  \"candidate_ranking\": [\n    \"insufficient_state_action_coverage\",\n    \"dynamics_distribution_shift\",\n    \"insufficient_model_capacity\",\n    \"optimization_instability\",\n    \"action_conditioning_failure\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_for\": [\n    \"Expanding n_episodes from 8 to 20 produced a substantial reduction in heldout error, with step_5_mse plummeting from 23.34 to 3.29, directly addressing the generalization gap.\",\n    \"One-step MSE improved from 9.54 to 8.04, and train_final_loss decreased from 0.78 to 0.26, confirming the model effectively utilized additional trajectory data without overfitting.\",\n    \"The EXP_COV experiment was explicitly designed to test whether increasing trajectory volume resolves the coverage bottleneck; the observed improvements strongly align with this hypothesis.\",\n    \"Normalized improvement of 0.157 indicates meaningful performance gains attributable to the expanded state-action sampling.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_against\": [\n    \"Optimization was stable and converged initially (lr_vs_typical_ratio=1.0, train_final_loss=0.78), ruling out optimization_instability as the primary failure mode.\",\n    \"The architecture (hidden_width=2, hidden_values=[32,32]) successfully minimized training loss on the initial dataset, indicating sufficient model capacity rather than structural limitations.\",\n    \"No indicators suggest a fundamental distributional mismatch driving dynamics_distribution_shift, as simple data expansion alone substantially closed the performance gap.\"\n  ],\n  \"missing_evidence\": [\n    \"Heldout performance under uniform or random action sampling to disentangle the effects of action bias from pure data quantity.\",\n    \"Longer-horizon rollout stability beyond step 10 to determine if error growth plateaus or continues to accelerate with increased coverage.\",\n    \"Ablation studies varying action_scale to quantify how much bias magnitude independently contributes to the coverage limitation.\"\n  ],\n  \"information_gain\": \"The experiment decisively validated insufficient_state_action_coverage as the primary bottleneck. Doubling the episode count significantly reduced the generalization gap and improved multi-step prediction accuracy, confirming that sparse, biased data collection—not model capacity or optimization instability—was limiting performance.\",\n  \"abstain\": false\n}",
